WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.800
Thanks for joining me on fascinating frontiers episode 109 for June 22nd, 2026.

00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:12.060
New week, new discoveries. Let's see what's happening. NASA's next flagship observatory has reached

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:18.540
Florida for final checks before an August launch on a Falcon Heavy. The $4.3 billion Nancy

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:25.380
Grace Roman Space Telescope touched down at Kennedy Space Center on June 21. It will spend roughly

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:31.920
70 days in pre-launch processing before riding a SpaceX Falcon Heavy no earlier than August 30.

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:38.140
The telescope's wide field infrared instrument is designed to serve a large sky areas for

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:43.980
exoplanets and dark energy measurements. Engineers will now integrate the spacecraft with its

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:51.220
launch adapter and run final environmental tests. The observatory will head to an L2 halo orbit once

00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:55.900
those checks are complete. On a completely different note, we turn to the company that now

00:00:55.900 --> 00:01:02.060
dominates getting satellites into orbit in the first place. SpaceX has now launched more satellites

00:01:02.060 --> 00:01:08.320
than all other providers combined throughout history. The company's cumulative orbital deliveries

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:14.380
exceed the total from every other launch operator to date. Reusable Falcon 9 first stages that

00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:21.300
return to landing platforms or ground pads after each flight drive the lead. No other operator has

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:27.340
matched the flight rate or payload capacity achieved so far. Future Starship flights could widen

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:34.000
the margin further once they enter regular service. While one operator scales quantity, another is

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:40.440
preparing hardware to bring materials back from orbit. Astroscale has secured new capital to move

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:46.480
from single demonstration flights to a sustained series of on-orbit servicing operations.

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:51.940
The fun support development of rendezvous and capture hardware for multiple clients,

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:57.740
servicing spacecraft could extend the life of commercial and government satellites already in orbit.

00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:03.100
The shift marks a step toward routine orbital maintenance rather than one-off tests.

00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:09.840
Next milestones include hardware qualification and customer contracts. Keeping satellites alive

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:15.980
is one challenge. Deciding what we might do with an entire planet is another. Researchers have

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:22.440
mapped the measurements needed to judge whether warming Mars is feasible. The plan focuses on releasing

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:27.940
greenhouse gases or deploying orbital mirrors to raise surface temperatures enough for liquid water.

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:34.000
Key unknowns include how much carbon dioxide remains trapped in the polar caps and regolith.

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:40.300
Data from current orbiters and future landers will feed into climate models that test each scenario.

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:45.080
The study provides a clear checklist rather than claiming success as assured.

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:50.940
Before we alter another world, we're learning how Earth life behaves in Mars-like conditions.

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:57.000
Experiments expose microorganisms to low pressure, cold temperatures, and radiation levels

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:02.880
modeled on the Martian surface. Several strains remained viable after the exposure period.

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:08.780
The work also found that the same microbes triggered weaker immune responses in test subjects.

00:03:09.220 --> 00:03:15.500
Results suggest that any future sample return hardware must account for hardy terrestrial contaminants.

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:20.200
The findings apply equally to protecting Earth from potential Martian material.

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:27.400
While labs study microbes, a new commercial capsule aims to manufacture materials in orbit and return

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:32.800
them. SpaceX is preparing a dis-shaped reentry vehicle for a demonstration flight.

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:38.040
The capsule is sized to return small batches of materials processed in microgravity.

00:03:38.660 --> 00:03:44.300
No prior commercial reentry system has offered routine low-cost sample return from orbit.

00:03:45.080 --> 00:03:50.860
Engineers are targeting markets such as fiber optics and pharmaceuticals that benefit from extended

00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:56.880
weightless processing. A successful test would open a new class of orbital production missions.

00:03:57.400 --> 00:04:03.040
From new hardware in orbit, we shift to how operators already keep thousands of satellites safely

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:09.560
spaced. If every star-linked satellite were the size of a city bus, the shells they occupy would still

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:15.800
be separated by only a few tens of kilometers vertically. Ground operators upload a small thrust

00:04:15.800 --> 00:04:21.960
command days ahead when two satellites approach. The nudge moves one craft a few hundred meters

00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:28.440
aside so their paths never intersect. The adjustment uses less than a kilogram of propellant per year

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:33.500
per satellite because the relative velocities are modest and the warning time is long.

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:41.880
At 550 kilometers altitude, the orbital period is roughly 95 minutes, so each satellite passes over

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:48.540
any given ground point 15 times a day. The real puzzle is how operators will keep the same spacing

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:53.600
once thousands more satellites from competing constellations join the same altitude bands.

00:04:54.040 --> 00:04:59.560
We can already compute the probability of close approaches but no one yet knows the practical

00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:06.620
limit before active coordination becomes impossible. While city dwellers look up, communities once tied to

00:05:06.620 --> 00:05:12.800
coal are now building parts that go into orbit. The story follows how communities once centered on coal

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:19.080
extraction are now contributing components and talent to space programs. Local manufacturers supply

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:25.100
precision parts for launch vehicles and satellites. Workforce programs are retraining minors and

00:05:25.100 --> 00:05:31.960
engineers for aerospace roles. The transition illustrates one regional example of economic shifts tied

00:05:31.960 --> 00:05:40.240
to the space economy. More suppliers from the area are expected as launch demand grows. NGC 7,000 in

00:05:40.310 --> 00:05:46.990
Cygnus reaches nearly 70 degrees elevation in the eastern sky by 2 a.m. local daylight time.

00:05:47.910 --> 00:05:52.930
The emission nebula's shape resembles the continent of North America when viewed through narrow

00:05:52.930 --> 00:05:59.350
band filters. Observers with modest telescopes or binoculars can locate it by sweeping from

00:05:59.350 --> 00:06:05.650
Deneb toward the Cygnus star cloud. The object is a favorite summer target because it lies in a rich

00:06:05.650 --> 00:06:12.230
Milky Way field. Clear dark skies away from city lights give the best contrast. On Earth,

00:06:12.590 --> 00:06:17.210
ice is breaking up earlier in the bearing sea satellites are already tracking the change.

00:06:17.690 --> 00:06:23.630
Satellite views capture drifting ice fragments near St. Lawrence and Nunavac islands. Colorful sediment

00:06:23.630 --> 00:06:29.470
plumes around the Yukon Delta mark the seasonal transition. The imagery comes from instruments

00:06:29.470 --> 00:06:36.070
monitoring Arctic sea ice extent and ocean color. These observations help track how quickly open

00:06:36.070 --> 00:06:41.910
water appears each year. Continued monitoring will show whether the pattern repeats incoming seasons.

00:06:42.690 --> 00:06:50.390
The small jackal spacecraft launched aboard the SACE 500-2 mission on May 3. It joined a cluster

00:06:50.390 --> 00:06:56.270
of other secondary payloads released into low Earth orbit. Tracking data confirmed the satellite

00:06:56.270 --> 00:07:02.470
is operating as planned. Such ride share flights allow rapid deployment of technology demonstrators

00:07:02.470 --> 00:07:08.330
without dedicated rockets. Additional details on its mission objectives are still limited.

00:07:09.050 --> 00:07:15.530
The ad astra event runs September 1-3 and will gather input on the next generation of large

00:07:15.530 --> 00:07:22.050
astrophysics observatories. Participants will help define science priorities and technology needs.

00:07:22.690 --> 00:07:29.250
The workshop is organized by NASA's astrophysics division. Outcomes are expected to feed into

00:07:29.250 --> 00:07:36.330
the upcoming Decadal Survey process. Registration details appear on the Cosmic Origins program site.

00:07:36.950 --> 00:07:43.790
The IR Stig Prima series features Bogdan Pastrav of the Institute of Space Science on June 22.

00:07:44.730 --> 00:07:50.410
Separate AGM and Cosmic Origins sessions run later in the week with early career presenters.

00:07:51.050 --> 00:07:57.030
Topics range from Galaxy Evolution to new instrumentation concepts. The talks are open to

00:07:57.030 --> 00:08:02.270
the community via the PCAST webpage. Recordings will be archived for later viewing.

00:08:03.250 --> 00:08:09.230
A new commentary examines whether large language models will favor rapid but conventional results

00:08:09.230 --> 00:08:16.250
over novel ideas. Reviewers and funders may need new incentives to reward unexpected findings.

00:08:16.810 --> 00:08:21.570
The piece notes that model training data already reflect past publication patterns.

00:08:21.870 --> 00:08:26.190
Without deliberate changes, the diversity of scientific questions could narrow.

00:08:26.930 --> 00:08:30.650
The discussion continues among journal editors and funding agencies.

00:08:31.330 --> 00:08:36.010
Dissertation work tested how simulated space travel alters microbial behavior.

00:08:36.670 --> 00:08:42.410
Results indicate that some Earth organisms could persist on icy worlds where liquid water exists.

00:08:43.290 --> 00:08:47.670
The research also has implications for crew health on long-duration missions.

00:08:48.450 --> 00:08:53.950
Supervisors note the dual use of the data for both extraterrestrial and terrestrial applications.

00:08:54.510 --> 00:08:57.870
Follow-on experiments are planned with additional microbe strains.

